1
00:00:00,299 --> 00:00:02,881
Green tests don't always mean a safe release.

2
00:00:03,115 --> 00:00:06,977
I built ReleaseRadar to make testing evidence easier to understand.

3
00:00:07,125 --> 00:00:09,067
Watch this checkout example.

4
00:00:09,259 --> 00:00:10,945
The existing tests pass,

5
00:00:11,051 --> 00:00:13,991
but none combines a discount with shipping eligibility.

6
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:17,207
I load prepared cases and run them against both builds.

7
00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:18,871
Now the hidden bug appears:

8
00:00:18,977 --> 00:00:20,919
shipping uses the wrong subtotal.

9
00:00:21,068 --> 00:00:23,501
This bug is deliberately seeded for the demo.

10
00:00:23,671 --> 00:00:26,859
Next, I apply the fix and rerun the same assertions.

11
00:00:27,017 --> 00:00:28,788
The corrected build passes,

12
00:00:28,894 --> 00:00:31,519
while the original failures stay in the evidence.

13
00:00:31,796 --> 00:00:34,164
There are also API testing tools,

14
00:00:34,313 --> 00:00:36,895
Postman and OpenAPI imports,

15
00:00:37,044 --> 00:00:41,041
and an exploratory testing workbench with Jira integration flows.

16
00:00:41,155 --> 00:00:43,503
Live AI is still being configured.

17
00:00:43,630 --> 00:00:45,615
Try ReleaseRadar on Vercel,

18
00:00:45,721 --> 00:00:47,299
explore the code on GitHub,

19
00:00:47,299 --> 00:00:50,176
and tell me what would make your testing day easier.
